— Legacy Architecture Documentation
Why This Document Exists
This document preserves the architectural history of the Justicar / AxiomOS project from its earliest documented form through the evolution to the current kernel-level design. It is kept publicly because the evolution matters — not just the destination.
The current AxiomOS architecture is cleaner, more capable, and more structurally sound than what came before. That didn't happen by accident. It happened because each prior version was built, understood, and deliberately improved upon. The limitations of each iteration directly informed the design decisions that followed.
This is not a record of failure. It is a record of a system being designed correctly — starting from what was possible, learning what was necessary, and building toward what was always the goal.
The goal was always a constitutional kernel-level OS. The userspace implementations documented here were the proof-of-concept path to get there. They had to exist before the kernel architecture could be fully specified.
Document 1: Irwin File Tree — Legacy Userspace Architecture
Documented state: Early 2025. This represents the userspace proof of concept that informed the current kernel-level architecture.
Code
What This Was
This file tree represents Irwin — the first full implementation of the Justicar architecture — running as a userspace application on a standard Linux system. Every folder is a discrete system component: the constitutional governance layer, the CLI tools, the model routing engines, the knowledge graph, the academy, the sandbox test environment.
At its peak this system was running:
A fully connected MoE agent mesh across 3B, 8B, and 14B models
A knowledge graph with 496 nodes and over 5,000 edges
Autonomous LoRA training loops running unattended
A self-healing doctor script that scanned the bin and repaired broken paths
A teach command and teach-bash command for interactive learning
A meta-teacher generating its own training data
Dual data lake separation (prod lake / test lake / quarantine)
Hardware profiling tools
A full CLI with irwin, ia, idoc, ikg, isearch, and teach commands
Development was interrupted by hardware loss. The ❌ markers represent the state at the time of that loss — active development, not abandoned work.
Why the Architecture Evolved Past This
The userspace architecture worked. It proved the concepts. But it had a fundamental structural limitation that became clearer the more the system developed:
Every tool was a workaround for the absence of a natural language layer.
isearch existed because there was no other way to query the knowledge graph in plain English. The hardware profiler existed because there was no other way to express "what's wrong with my hardware." The self-healing script existed because there was no other way to tell the system to repair itself. Dozens of explicit CLI tools, each compensating for the same underlying gap — the system didn't understand intent, only commands.
When the natural language shell was identified as the correct architectural solution, approximately 90% of these tools became redundant. The tools were symptoms of a missing layer. The natural language shell treats the cause.
What Replaced It
The current AxiomOS architecture reduces the tool sprawl to six core components:
Constitutional Layer — the rules everything inherits
Natural Language Shell — plain English replaces explicit command tools
Dual Data Lake — public and private separation
Agora — federated governance and deliberation
Mastery Engine (Academy) — adaptive educational infrastructure
Axiom Coin — contribution tracking
The knowledge graph is queried through the natural language shell. The hardware profiler is replaced by a plain language conversation. The self-healing architecture is replaced by constitutional evaluation of system state. The teach command becomes the mastery engine. The constitutional layer moves from markdown files in /axiom/ to kernel-level enforcement.
The file tree above is not what AxiomOS will look like. It is what AxiomOS had to look like before the kernel path was clear.

